#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144074 :tutorial/content "The **reciprocal** of a fraction is a bit like turning it upside down: we switch the numerator and denominator.\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a $\\dfrac{1}{20},$ we switch the numerator and the denominator as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}20} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}20}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20} 1 = 20\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{1}{20}$ is $20.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "db280674-4a34-5dbd-99a3-c1c2c0ecf8be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8576 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144075 :tutorial/content "To find the reciprocal of a whole number, we must first write the number as a fraction.\n\nFor instance, let's write the number $2$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n2= \\dfrac{2} 1\n$$\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}1} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of $2$ is $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "439c60e5-dcf3-50b0-bc8f-a2d2ce74cdcc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8577 :tutorial/title "The Reciprocal of a Whole Number"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144076 :tutorial/content "To find the reciprocal of a mixed number, we must first convert it to an improper fraction.\n\nLet's find the reciprocal of $1\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}.$ First, we convert the given mixed number to an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{(1\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}5}{\\color{red}3} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of $1\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}$ is $\\dfrac{3}{5}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ae66510a-0eed-5ee9-8a6e-20d52291d930" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8578 :tutorial/title "The Reciprocal of a Mixed Number"}]}
